{"abstract":"In this article we develop the concept of addressability to help us unpack processes of identification. We start from a foundational sociological account of addressing as laid out by Simmel, and use Luhmann’s systems theory to identify tensions in the overlap between different systems. The dual character of addressing as reductive (in meaning) and constructive (of communicative positions) helps us understand a mode of knowledge production that generates its own recipients. By concentrating on the moment of addressing in this manner and developing the concept of addressability to explain its complexity, we seek to build an analytic concept that is useful for scholars who are interested in unpacking the construction of communicative positions in identification. We demonstrate the potential of this concept with an analysis of two moments of addressability in action that involve personal identification numbers. We conclude that the intersection and mutual challenge of these two approaches can help us connect different addressing moments while also moving beyond questions of surveillance and entitlement that routinely seek to capture the problem of identification.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Cakici, Baki","Thiel, Alena"],"cuid":["S02-091.43-822883"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Identification","Surveillance","Systems theory"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"addressability i identification and communicative positions in critical sociological perspective i","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.37062/sf.62.27828","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-091.43-822883","identifier":[{"id":"105008954431","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f5fb7355-a893-46cb-9494-f6514318100b","ku-40601547-2182-419d-a44c-f771bc590b5c"],"keyword":["Addressability","Identification","Identification numbers","Surveillance","Systems theory"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-B396EF3C666D4455A0F8785132098970"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.37062/sf.62.27828","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/461831411/07_SoFo_nr_1_2_2025_cakici_1_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, ETHOS Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Theology, Centre for African Studies - Technical area, Centre for African Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Baki","key":"cakicipau","last":"Cakici","locid":"b614ac6f-eefa-44a3-8b9a-092b88d86d37","name":"Cakici, Baki","orcid":"0000-0002-1039-8177","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Alena","key":"thielpau","last":"Thiel","locid":"f240d002-893f-45bd-b9ad-6a6ddc015e40","name":"Thiel, Alena","orcid":"0000-0002-1092-1140","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.37062/sf.62.27828","issn":["00380342"],"issue":"1-2","journal":"Sociologisk Forskning","pages":"107-120","status":"p","title":"Sociologisk Forskning","volume":"62"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105008954431"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f5fb7355-a893-46cb-9494-f6514318100b","ku-40601547-2182-419d-a44c-f771bc590b5c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Addressability: Identification and Communicative Positions in Critical Sociological Perspective","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-091.43-822883","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"In the early 2000s, authorities in Sweden and Denmark recognised that their personal identification numbers were about to run out but followed different interventions to resolve the same issue. In this paper, I start from these cases to analyse personal identification numbers as methods for knowing and governing populations. I draw on two assertions from the study of methods within STS: Methods are performative, and they produce multiple objects and realities. I demonstrate how such identification numbers enact individuals and populations simultaneously, and I identify a fundamental tension between them: one emphasising the representational potential of the part and another favouring the coherence of the whole. I conclude that issues surrounding personal identification numbers in use across all Nordic countries can be traced back to a fundamental tension in addressing individuals that is impossible to resolve via technical fixes, although those interventions are crucial to keeping the systems operational.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Cakici, Baki"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776385"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Civil Registration","Identification","Methods","Numbers","Performativity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"when numbers run out civil registration and the performativity of methods","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102855084/127395-Article_Text-321226-1-10-20240119.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776385","identifier":[{"id":"85212702669","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-30523062-e4a6-4ae1-9d1b-140cbfb86c6b"],"keyword":["Civil Registration","Identification","Methods","Numbers","Performativity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-A6B1656CF842E3CB7D5D85E7653B114A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102855084/127395-Article_Text-321226-1-10-20240119.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, ETHOS Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Baki","key":"cakicipau","last":"Cakici","locid":"b614ac6f-eefa-44a3-8b9a-092b88d86d37","name":"Cakici, Baki","orcid":"0000-0002-1039-8177","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["22434690"],"issue":"4","journal":"Science & Technology Studies","pages":"62-76","publisher":"Finnish Society for Science and Technology Studies","status":"p","title":"Science & Technology Studies","volume":"37"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85212702669"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-30523062-e4a6-4ae1-9d1b-140cbfb86c6b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"When Numbers Run Out : Civil Registration and the Performativity of Methods","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776385","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
