{"abstract":"On the eve of the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) coming into effect we, a university laboratory, marked the occasion with an interactive installation called Compliance. Data traces from Compliance were subsequently processed by the lab, here enacted in the form of a play. While much discussion has centered around modern 'black-boxed' processing of data, less attention has been paid to the value of the data itself, and whether it merits use. We draw on dramaturgical methods for both analysis and presentation [15], allowing for readers to imagine staging their own, different, versions of the event. Drawing on the ambiguous ontological status of (yet unexamined) data, we offer a discussion on the value of data, its use and non-use, as well as how to live with this ambivalence, continuously negotiating social contracts about our further conduct with the data.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","Ferreira, Pedro","Cohn, Marisa","Henriksen, Line","Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","Hockenhull, Michael","Cakici, Baki","Blønd, Marie","Douglas-Jones, Rachel","Laursen, Cæcilie Sloth","Zell, Sonja"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384849"],"event":{"acronym":"CHI","title":"ACM Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["GDPR","data non use","dramaturgical methods"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"upon not opening the black box","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85090243080","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384849","identifier":[{"id":"85090243080","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f39b7fd5-7493-4bf3-a0f8-14dc03ecc282"],"keyword":["GDPR","data non-use","dramaturgical methods"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-276723FEC91BFD0CA307B03D82CF6C55"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Simy Kaur","key":"gahooniapau","last":"Gahoonia","locid":"6c543b1d-8137-489d-b444-d17921fc9de9","name":"Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","orcid":"0000-0001-9668-6868","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pedro","key":"ferreirapau","last":"Ferreira","locid":"1b3cff7f-1549-4998-b52e-0ba9093e1d01","name":"Ferreira, Pedro","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","name":"Henriksen, Line","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Katrine Meldgaard","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"0c46864b-a7f8-4439-aeda-0781f77bfe88","name":"Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","orcid":"0000-0001-7994-5186","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"hockenhullpau","last":"Hockenhull","locid":"5041f387-908f-4845-9f79-3a6dbd527f4b","name":"Hockenhull, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-2670-2102","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Baki","key":"cakicipau","last":"Cakici","locid":"b614ac6f-eefa-44a3-8b9a-092b88d86d37","name":"Cakici, Baki","orcid":"0000-0002-1039-8177","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marie","key":"bløndpau","last":"Blønd","name":"Blønd, Marie","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Cæcilie Sloth","key":"laursenpau","last":"Laursen","locid":"3ef9211c-34b2-4061-ba59-c5800d6a5b32","name":"Laursen, Cæcilie Sloth","orcid":"0000-0001-7102-7268","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Sonja","key":"zellpau","last":"Zell","name":"Zell, Sonja","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3334480.3381813","eisbn":["9781450368193/20/04"],"pages":"1-9","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI ’20 Extended Abstracts"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85090243080"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f39b7fd5-7493-4bf3-a0f8-14dc03ecc282"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Upon Not Opening The Black Box","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384849","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Politically, Europe has been unable to address itself to a constituted polity and people as more than an agglomeration of nation-states. From the resurgence of nationalisms to the crisis of the single currency and the unprecedented decision of a member state to leave the European Union (EU), core questions about the future of Europe have been rearticulated: Who are the people of Europe? Is there a European identity? What does it mean to say, “I am European?” Where does Europe begin and end? and Who can legitimately claim to be a part of a “European” people? The special issue (SI) seeks to contest dominant framings of the question “Who are the people of Europe?” as only a matter of government policies, electoral campaigns, or parliamentary debates. Instead, the contributions start from the assumption that answers to this question exist in data practices where people are addressed, framed, known, and governed as European. The central argument of this SI is that it is through data practices that the EU seeks to simultaneously constitute its population as a knowable, governable entity, and as a distinct form of peoplehood where common personhood is more important than differences.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Cakici, Baki","Ruppert, Evelyn","Scheel, Stephan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384919"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Goldsmiths, University of London"],"keyword":["Big data","Digital technology","Method","Official Statistics","Subjectivation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"peopling europe through data practices introduction to the special issue","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/0162243919897822","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384919","identifier":[{"id":"85077155166","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f7f85270-9369-47f9-8be7-688224ae94ff"],"keyword":["Big Data","digital technology","method","official statistics","subjectivation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6F938BF01E8DF6EF83B1C72786768446"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84834489/Methods_as_forces_of_subjectivation_experiments_in_the_remaking_of_official_statistics.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/0162243919897822","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"goldsmiths university of london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Goldsmiths, University of London","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Baki","key":"cakicipau","last":"Cakici","locid":"b614ac6f-eefa-44a3-8b9a-092b88d86d37","name":"Cakici, Baki","orcid":"0000-0002-1039-8177","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Evelyn","key":"ruppertpau","last":"Ruppert","name":"Ruppert, Evelyn","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Stephan","key":"scheelpau","last":"Scheel","name":"Scheel, Stephan","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/0162243919897822","issn":["01622439","15528251"],"issn_org":["01622439"],"journal":"Science, Technology & Human Values","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Science, Technology & Human Values"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85077155166"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f7f85270-9369-47f9-8be7-688224ae94ff"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Peopling Europe through Data Practices: Introduction to the Special Issue","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384919","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Beck and Sznaider call on ‘methodological cosmopolitanism’ to transcend methodological nationalism and account for an increasingly cosmopolitanized reality. We take up their challenge by drawing on our experiences of conducting a collaborative ethnography of methodological changes in the production of population statistics within and between European national and international statistical institutes. Drawing on debates in science and technology studies, we depart from some conceptual presuppositions of methodological cosmopolitanism to define a ‘transversal method’. Referring to this method as performative and ontopolitical, we reflect on how it requires collaboration and, in our ethnography, gave rise to three practical challenges – (1) going beyond the individual project; (2) using each other's field notes; (3) and working against the national order of things. To meet these challenges, we reflect on how this method required us to practise three modes of care – thinking with others, tinkering with field notes, and dissenting within.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Scheel, Stephan","Grommé, Francisca","Ruppert, Evelyn","Ustek-Spilda, Funda","Cakici, Baki","Takala, Ville"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381462"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Goldsmiths, University of London","The London School of Economics and Political Science","University College London","University of Duisburg-Essen"],"keyword":["CARE","Collaboration","Cosmopolitanism","Ethnography","ONTOPOLITICAL","Performativity","TRANSVERSAL METHOD"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"doing a transversal method developing an ethics of care in a collaborative research project","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/glob.12263","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381462","identifier":[{"id":"85071777773","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-0d175005-29c0-46ec-bc10-e1da780a0b3c"],"keyword":["CARE","COLLABORATION","COSMOPOLITANISM","ETHNOGRAPHY","ONTOPOLITICAL","PERFORMATIVITY","TRANSVERSAL METHOD"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-41D5F1BFD6161BED7378AC06C9A4A603"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/glob.12263","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84729464/SCHEEL_et_al_2019_Global_Networks.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"university of duisburg essen","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Duisburg-Essen","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"goldsmiths university of london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Goldsmiths, University of London","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"the london school of economics and political science","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"The London School of Economics and Political Science","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"gb","key":"university college london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University College London","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Stephan","key":"scheelpau","last":"Scheel","name":"Scheel, Stephan","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Francisca","key":"grommépau","last":"Grommé","name":"Grommé, Francisca","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Evelyn","key":"ruppertpau","last":"Ruppert","name":"Ruppert, Evelyn","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Funda","key":"ustekspildapau","last":"Ustek-Spilda","name":"Ustek-Spilda, Funda","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Baki","key":"cakicipau","last":"Cakici","locid":"b614ac6f-eefa-44a3-8b9a-092b88d86d37","name":"Cakici, Baki","orcid":"0000-0002-1039-8177","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Ville","key":"takalapau","last":"Takala","name":"Takala, Ville","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/glob.12263","issn":["14702266","14710374"],"issn_org":["14702266"],"journal":"Global Networks: A Journal of Transnational Affairs","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","status":"p","title":"Global Networks: A Journal of Transnational Affairs"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85071777773"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-0d175005-29c0-46ec-bc10-e1da780a0b3c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Doing a transversal method: developing an ethics of care in a collaborative research project","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381462","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Critical algorithm scholarship has demonstrated the difficulties of attributing accountability for the actions and effects of algorithmic systems. In this commentary, we argue that we cannot stop at denouncing the lack of accountability for algorithms and their effects but must engage the broader systems and distributed agencies that algorithmic systems exist within; including standards, regulations, technologies, and social relations. To this end, we explore accountability in “the Generated Detective,” an algorithmically generated comic. Taking up the mantle of detectives ourselves, we investigate accountability in relation to this piece of experimental fiction. We problematize efforts to effect accountability through transparency by undertaking a simple operation: asking for permission to re-publish a set of the algorithmically selected and modified words and images which make the frames of the comic. Recounting this process, we demonstrate slippage between the “complication” of the algorithm and the obscurity of the legal and institutional structures in which it exists.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Reddy, Elizabeth","Cakici, Baki","Ballestero, Andrea"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383000"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Colorado School of Mines","Rice University"],"keyword":["Accountability","Algorithms","Mystery","Normativity","Responsibility","detective"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"beyond mystery putting algorithmic accountability in context","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/2053951719826856","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383000","identifier":[{"id":"85074177160","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-7581c846-6294-4876-bd35-6f909d10c854"],"keyword":["Algorithms","accountability","detective","mystery","normativity","responsibility"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6F6E62F136D798A965911D78510624CB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/2053951719826856","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83876769/2053951719826856.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"colorado school of mines","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Colorado School of Mines","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"rice university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Rice University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Elizabeth","key":"reddypau","last":"Reddy","name":"Reddy, Elizabeth","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Baki","key":"cakicipau","last":"Cakici","locid":"b614ac6f-eefa-44a3-8b9a-092b88d86d37","name":"Cakici, Baki","orcid":"0000-0002-1039-8177","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrea","key":"ballesteropau","last":"Ballestero","name":"Ballestero, Andrea","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/2053951719826856","issn":["20539517"],"issue":"1","journal":"Big Data & Society","pages":"1-7","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Big Data & Society","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85074177160"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7581c846-6294-4876-bd35-6f909d10c854"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Beyond mystery: Putting algorithmic accountability in context","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383000","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"We develop the concept of methods as ‘forces of subjectivation’ in relation to experiments we have encountered in a study of government methods for generating official population statistics. These experiments problematise the subjects of traditional methods based on paper questionnaires and offer new digital technologies and data sources as possible solutions. We reflect on these experiments in relation to recent work on sociological and digital research methods as inventive and live. What this work identifies in relation to questions of research methods we take up to think about government methods in two ways. One concerns how government method experiments offered as solutions to problematic subjects, once put into action, change initial problem formulations and are inventive of new ones. Secondly, they are also inventive of their subjects who do not pre-exist but come into being through the agential capacities that methods configure. Both aspects of methods, we argue, are the result of the interactions and dynamics between human and technological actors, the outcomes of which cannot be settled in advance.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Cakici, Baki","Ruppert, Evelyn"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384587"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Goldsmiths, University of London"],"keyword":["Big data","Digital technology","Method","Official Statistics","Subjectivation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"methods as forces of subjectivation experiments in the remaking of official statistics","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84703998/Methods_as_forces_of_subjectivation_experiments_in_the_remaking_of_official_statistics.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384587","identifier":[{"id":"85075810675","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-e15bec00-7cba-487b-abae-5cef4a4814cd"],"keyword":["big data","digital technology","method","official statistics","subjectivation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-EC9E2731ABF9B6EB4BF659A4DCF37614"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84703998/Methods_as_forces_of_subjectivation_experiments_in_the_remaking_of_official_statistics.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"goldsmiths university of london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Goldsmiths, University of London","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Baki","key":"cakicipau","last":"Cakici","locid":"b614ac6f-eefa-44a3-8b9a-092b88d86d37","name":"Cakici, Baki","orcid":"0000-0002-1039-8177","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Evelyn","key":"ruppertpau","last":"Ruppert","name":"Ruppert, Evelyn","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/17530350.2019.1684340","issn":["17530350","17530369"],"issn_org":["17530350"],"journal":"Journal of Cultural Economy","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","title":"Journal of Cultural Economy"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85075810675"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e15bec00-7cba-487b-abae-5cef4a4814cd"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Methods as forces of subjectivation: experiments in the remaking of official statistics","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384587","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
